  planner_response {
    response: "To connect the **Register Now** button and registration form to your Excel or Google Sheets spreadsheet, there are **three reliable methods**:\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: Free Webhook with Google Sheets / Excel (Recommended & Easiest)\n\nYou can set up a free Google Apps Script web app (or Microsoft Power Automate flow for OneDrive Excel) that automatically appends every new registration as a new row in your spreadsheet.\n\n#### Step 1: Set Up the Spreadsheet Webhook\n1. Open your Google Sheet (or Excel connected to OneDrive).\n2. Set up headers in Row 1: `Timestamp`, `Pass ID`, `Full Name`, `Email`, `Phone`, `College/Org`, `Rating Type`, `Rating Value`.\n3. In Google Sheets, go to **Extensions** > **Apps Script** and paste this short script:\n\n```javascript\nfunction doPost(e) {\n  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();\n  var data = JSON.parse(e.postData.contents);\n  \n  sheet.appendRow([\n    new Date(),\n    data.passId,\n    data.fullName,\n    data.email,\n    data.phone,\n    data.collegeOrOrg,\n    data.ratingType,\n    data.ratingValue || \'Unrated\'\n  ]);\n  \n  return ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify({ status: \'success\' }))\n    .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);\n}\n```\n\n4. Click **Deploy** > **New deployment** > Select **Web app**.\n5. Set *Who has access* to **Anyone**.\n6. Copy the **Web App URL** provided.\n\n#### Step 2: Trigger on Form Submission\nWhen the player submits the registration form in the app, the form sends a `fetch` request to your Web App URL with the player data.\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: No-Code Webhook Tools (Zapier / Make / SheetDB)\nIf you prefer Microsoft 365 Excel directly:\n1. Create a free webhook on **Make.com**, **Zapier**, or **SheetDB.io**.\n2. Set the trigger to *Catch Webhook* and the action to *Microsoft Excel Online - Add Row to Worksheet*.\n3. Use the webhook URL in the app\'s registration submission handler.\n\n---\n\n### Method 3: One-Click Excel / CSV Organizer Export\nWe can also add an **\"Export Registrations to Excel (.xlsx / .csv)\"** button directly into the app so you can download all registrations at any time.\n\n---\n\n### Would you like me to implement this for you?\nIf you\'d like, I can:\n1. Add a **Webhook / Google Sheets URL configuration** in the app so any submissions are posted to your spreadsheet in real time.\n2. Add a **CSV / Excel export button** for organizers to download player lists instantly."
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Intent**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the user\'s question. My primary aim is to understand what the user truly wants to know. I\'m referencing informational question guidelines to determine the core intent: the user\'s need for understanding. This will inform my next steps.\n\n\n**Clarifying Actionable Steps**\n\nI\'ve outlined specific actionable steps to connect a registration form to Excel or Google Sheets, starting with the most popular method: using webhook APIs, such as Google Apps Script or Microsoft Power Automate, with an HTTP POST request. I\'ll clearly illustrate how the form data (name, email, etc.) can be sent to a webhook URL, where it is appended to a sheet as a new row in real-time.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "To connect the **Register Now** button and registration form to your Excel or Google Sheets spreadsheet, there are **three reliable methods**:\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: Free Webhook with Google Sheets / Excel (Recommended & Easiest)\n\nYou can set up a free Google Apps Script web app (or Microsoft Power Automate flow for OneDrive Excel) that automatically appends every new registration as a new row in your spreadsheet.\n\n#### Step 1: Set Up the Spreadsheet Webhook\n1. Open your Google Sheet (or Excel connected to OneDrive).\n2. Set up headers in Row 1: `Timestamp`, `Pass ID`, `Full Name`, `Email`, `Phone`, `College/Org`, `Rating Type`, `Rating Value`.\n3. In Google Sheets, go to **Extensions** > **Apps Script** and paste this short script:\n\n```javascript\nfunction doPost(e) {\n  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();\n  var data = JSON.parse(e.postData.contents);\n  \n  sheet.appendRow([\n    new Date(),\n    data.passId,\n    data.fullName,\n    data.email,\n    data.phone,\n    data.collegeOrOrg,\n    data.ratingType,\n    data.ratingValue || \'Unrated\'\n  ]);\n  \n  return ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify({ status: \'success\' }))\n    .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);\n}\n```\n\n4. Click **Deploy** > **New deployment** > Select **Web app**.\n5. Set *Who has access* to **Anyone**.\n6. Copy the **Web App URL** provided.\n\n#### Step 2: Trigger on Form Submission\nWhen the player submits the registration form in the app, the form sends a `fetch` request to your Web App URL with the player data.\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: No-Code Webhook Tools (Zapier / Make / SheetDB)\nIf you prefer Microsoft 365 Excel directly:\n1. Create a free webhook on **Make.com**, **Zapier**, or **SheetDB.io**.\n2. Set the trigger to *Catch Webhook* and the action to *Microsoft Excel Online - Add Row to Worksheet*.\n3. Use the webhook URL in the app\'s registration submission handler.\n\n---\n\n### Method 3: One-Click Excel / CSV Organizer Export\nWe can also add an **\"Export Registrations to Excel (.xlsx / .csv)\"** button directly into the app so you can download all registrations at any time.\n\n---\n\n### Would you like me to implement this for you?\nIf you\'d like, I can:\n1. Add a **Webhook / Google Sheets URL configuration** in the app so any submissions are posted to your spreadsheet in real time.\n2. Add a **CSV / Excel export button** for organizers to download player lists instantly."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 1
      nanos: 210082196
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
